  "narrative_body": "Beijing's preferred peace formula came with a blunt rejection attached this week, as Taiwan's president declared that the \"peace through unification\" framework being advanced through the island's main opposition party would bring Taiwan \"endless trouble\" — a pointed counter-move timed precisely as Washington prepares to meet China at the highest level.\n\nLai Ching-te delivered that rebuke at a ruling DPP party meeting on April 15, mobilizing the party apparatus rather than just the executive to frame the KMT's cross-strait diplomacy as threat rather than opportunity. The Institute for the Study of War's reconstruction of the Beijing talks deepened the concern: KMT Chairwoman Cheng Li-wun reportedly echoed PRC rhetoric on \"national rejuvenation\" during her meeting with Xi Jinping, and Beijing is now conditioning continued cooperation with the KMT on acceptance of that framing. Taiwan's Mainland Affairs Council noted that Ms Cheng failed to raise a requested talking point demanding that China stop its military aggression. A separate row broke out over whether the Taiwan Foundation for Democracy, funded by the foreign ministry, had reimbursed KMT travel costs to Beijing. Nikkei offered a competing interpretation: that Xi accepted the meeting partly because PLA military purges had eroded his confidence in military options, making party-to-party diplomacy a substitute for coercive leverage he can no longer fully exercise.\n\nEven as those arguments played out domestically, the US Congress moved to insulate Taiwan's security relationship from the Trump administration's diplomatic maneuvering. Four senators — two from each party — published a letter addressed directly to Taiwan's legislative speaker and all three caucuses, assuring them that pending weapons sales, including counter-drone assets, an integrated battle command system, and medium-range air defense munitions, would be announced within weeks. They explicitly timed the letter ahead of the American president's planned May 14-15 visit to China, a signal that Congress would not let summit-driven concessions interrupt the arms pipeline. On April 17, Mr Lai reinforced the message at a military inspection attended by NSC Secretary-General Joseph Wu and Defense Minister Wellington Koo, calling for the NT$1.25 trillion special defense budget to pass without cuts. In a separate step, Taiwan's National Security Bureau announced it will directly support military operations during this year's Han Kuang exercises for the first time — a shift from advisory to operational integration that extends the \"whole-of-society defense\" concept from doctrine to practice.\n\nAgainst that political turbulence, TSMC delivered results that reminded the world why Taiwan matters structurally. The company posted a net profit of NT$572.5 billion (US$18.2 billion) for the first quarter — up 58% year-on-year and the 8th consecutive quarter of double-digit growth. CEO C.C. Wei raised full-year revenue growth guidance to above 30% in dollar terms and lifted capital spending to the high end of the $52-56 billion range. The more significant threshold, though, was technological: TSMC has entered 2nm mass production and is expanding 3nm capacity simultaneously across Taiwan, Arizona, and Japan. Revenue from 3nm chips alone reached 25% of total sales, up from 6% in the third quarter of 2023. Mr Wei acknowledged supply-chain risks from the Middle East conflict — helium and hydrogen inputs — but said safety stocks and multiple suppliers keep production secure. Ahead of his Eswatini visit later this month, Mr Lai travels with substantive deliverables including a strategic oil reserve facility, an investment zone, and a digital technology partnership — the kind of economic statecraft Taiwan deploys to hold together its shrinking network of 12 formal allies.\n\nThe week's most analytically pointed finding came from a Reuters investigation tracing China's domestic information campaign against the Lai government. Rather than fabricating content, Chinese state media — including a CCP-run Douyin account — are amplifying authentic Taiwanese voices: KMT politicians and social media influencers whose criticism of Mr Lai they spread across Douyin, Facebook, YouTube, and TikTok. The campaign was traced to China's warship drills in December 2025. The method matters: amplified real content is harder to counter than fabricated posts, and it fuses the KMT-Beijing diplomatic engagement with a domestic delegitimization effort, turning opposition politics into an instrument of foreign pressure.\n\nOn the legislative front, a six-month budget impasse showed its first crack. All three caucuses agreed on April 18 to send the FY2026 general budget to committee review, conditional on Premier Cho Jung-tai appearing before lawmakers on April 21. The agreement also requires the Cabinet to release NT$71.8 billion in already-approved spending and commit to bills on military pay and public-safety pensions within six months — though neither condition carries an enforcement mechanism. As of Saturday, Mr Cho had not confirmed his attendance. The KMT simultaneously demanded a briefing on the government's plan to recruit Indian migrant workers, citing a 35,000-signature petition and concerns about 93,000 migrants already absent from the system. Labor Minister Hung confirmed the first 1,000 Indian workers could arrive this year, adding a new friction point to a legislature that has barely found its footing.",
